items: js-long-arith-no-64bit-wrap
paths: file:v2/backend/check.sh file:v2/conformance/bigint-dynamic-arith.coreir file:v2/backend/jvm/JvmBackend.scala
scope: |
  `js-long-arith-no-64bit-wrap` says the residue is the v2 JS emitter missing v1's `isLongExpr`
  discriminator. MEASURED BEFORE CLAIMING, on the emitted bundle: that is not the mechanism.
  `BigInt(1000000000)` reaches JS as the literal `1000000000n` — `JsBackend.scala:129` renders
  `Lit(CBig)` byte-identically to `Lit(CInt)` on line 128 — and `p * b` reaches it as
  `$arith("*",…)`, which masks to 64 bits. CoreIR DOES carry the tag (`Const.CBig`,
  `v2/src/CoreIR.scala:12`) and the VM keeps it (`Runtime.scala:1245` → `BigV`); the generator
  discards it. v1's discriminator cannot port, because v1 had front-end types and CoreIR has none.

  A CoreIR fixture put the question to every backend at once, and the answer widened the entry:
  all FOUR source generators fail it, not the JS one.

    jvm   row 1 prints, rows 2-4 do not — it THROWS
    js    rows 2-3 truncated to the 64-bit answer
    rust  rows 1-3 wrong (the `V::Int(i64)` bignum gap, already filed)
    wasm  same as rust

  NARROW, and deliberately only the half that is a small complete fix: the JVM generator already
  KEEPS the tag (`JvmBackend.scala:1147` emits `BigInt("n"): V`, a real `scala.math.BigInt`), so its
  `_arith` is missing one dispatch arm and nothing else. JS needs a representational change — 21
  `typeof …==='bigint'` sites and 40 `BigInt(` uses in one file — and is NOT attempted here; it
  stays open with the corrected mechanism written down.

  WIDENED to `v2/backend/jvm/JvmBackend.scala` BEFORE the edit, not after.
next: add the `(BigInt, BigInt)` arm to `_arith`, then skip js/rust/wasm with named reasons
